        "note": "Finding #64: this file's real subject -- mesh continuity across the landscape/crater junction -- can't be captured by a structured state field (no 'gap count' or 'seam detected' exists in SerializableGameState), so `expect.equals` here asserts what IS structurally verifiable (world bounds stay constant at 48x48 from (0,0) throughout, proving nothing shifts or resizes; holeCount/chargedCount/sequencedCount through the drill/charge/sequence/blast cycle; ecology/nuisance after the blast) while the continuity claim itself was verified the way a rendering claim should be: ran interaction mode with --screenshots and inspected step-07-drill_plan-ss0.png (pre-blast, Grid Tool panel showing the real panel defaults -- spacing 3.0m, depth 6.0m, 89mm diameter, confirming the drag genuinely produces the declared 2x2 grid) against step-10-blast.png (post-blast, same camera framing) with the Read tool directly. The fresh crater (a lighter, exposed-rock patch) blends into the surrounding terrain with a naturally jagged boundary, no visible gap, floating chunk, or hard-edged material snap across the junction -- issue #491's fix holds. Also confirmed the file's own multi-angle 'shots' (overview/birdseye) capture a fixed world-relative camera framing (unrelated to the drill site's specific coordinates), so those specific shots show general ridge/slope terrain rather than the crater itself -- the crater comparison came from the per-step default screenshots, not the named shots. Only spacing needed correcting in this file's single `drill_plan` step (4->3, matching the panel's real default) -- the declared 2x2 hole count already matched what a spacing-3 drag of this size produces, so this was a cosmetic-accuracy fix (Finding #52's class) rather than a hole-count-changing one. Verified in both command mode and a real browser with screenshots, 13/13 steps, 0 failures."
